WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.158
Dairy is one of the most data-rich livestock sectors.

2
00:00:04.208 --> 00:00:07.832
Herd records, milking systems, sensors, feed software.

3
00:00:07.882 --> 00:00:14.246
Yet sustainability only improves when those data become decisions a farm can actually carry out.

4
00:00:14.646 --> 00:00:20.118
This review argues that the path from data to decision is a pipeline with five layers:

5
00:00:20.168 --> 00:00:24.878
data foundations, governance, analytics, decision engines, and deployment.

6
00:00:24.928 --> 00:00:27.353
A weak layer anywhere breaks the chain.

7
00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:31.045
Layer one is data foundations.

8
00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:36.847
Integration usually fails not because data are missing, but because systems disagree:

9
00:00:36.897 --> 00:00:42.176
on cow identifiers, on timestamps, on units, on how often things are recorded.

10
00:00:42.226 --> 00:00:50.137
The fix is engineered ingestion, harmonization and quality control, delivering stable products such as cow-day tables.

11
00:00:50.537 --> 00:00:52.060
Layer two is governance.

12
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:57.028
Who owns the data, who may use it, for what, and how consent can be withdrawn.

13
00:00:57.078 --> 00:01:01.367
Without clear answers, farms stop sharing, and the pipeline dries up.

14
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:04.700
Layer three is analytics.

15
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.967
Machine learning turns raw streams into signals:

16
00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:10.633
risk scores, phenotypes, anomaly flags.

17
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:15.766
One example predicted mastitis early, catching eighty-five percent of cases.

18
00:01:15.816 --> 00:01:22.231
But such models struggle to transfer between herds, and a prediction alone is not yet a decision.

19
00:01:23.881 --> 00:01:27.007
Layer four, decision engines, closes that gap.

20
00:01:27.057 --> 00:01:32.413
Optimization asks what we should do, given explicit objectives and constraints.

21
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:39.981
In one study it cut feed cost by two dollars fifty-two per cow per day, or enteric methane by fifty-seven grams.

22
00:01:40.381 --> 00:01:45.785
Simulation asks what will happen, carrying biology, time and uncertainty forward.

23
00:01:45.835 --> 00:01:53.301
Calibrating lactation curves cut the error in simulated herd milk from forty point six percent, to two point two.

24
00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:56.189
A digital twin goes one step further:

25
00:01:56.239 --> 00:02:04.873
a farm model that is continuously updated from live data, recalibrated, and re-run for new what-if scenarios as conditions change.

26
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:08.146
Layer five is deployment.

27
00:02:08.196 --> 00:02:14.452
Validate for the intended use, with forward-in-time tests, external herds and calibration checks.

28
00:02:14.502 --> 00:02:19.085
Monitor for drift in the data, in the relationships, and in the labels.

29
00:02:19.135 --> 00:02:22.110
And measure decision impact, not just accuracy.

30
00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.611
The review ends with a staged roadmap.

31
00:02:26.661 --> 00:02:31.763
In the first six months, define the decisions, and get data and governance ready.

32
00:02:31.813 --> 00:02:35.659
By eighteen months, validate analytics inside real workflows.

33
00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:40.309
By three years, couple them to decision engines and digital-twin practice.

34
00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:41.929
The main gap today:

35
00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:48.211
few studies connect prediction, a decision engine, and real deployment in one farm-facing workflow.

36
00:02:50.611 --> 00:02:51.364
The takeaway:

37
00:02:51.414 --> 00:02:54.977
value comes from the whole pipeline, not from any single model.
